WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Bash scripts receive command-line arguments as positional parameters.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:14.200
The script name is represented separately from the arguments, individual values can be accessed by position, and the total number of supplied arguments can be checked before work begins.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.220
These details are not trivia.

4
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.480
They determine how the script understands its input and whether it can reject an incomplete or unsupported request.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.360
Argument count is a simple but powerful control.

6
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.900
If a script requires a mode and a destination, it should check that both are present before taking action.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.520
If a script supports no extra positional values, it should reject unexpected extras instead of silently ignoring them.

8
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:45.220
Clear count checks make usage predictable and keep mistakes from flowing deeper into the workflow.

9
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.820
The full argument list also matters.

10
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:56.960
In Bash, quoted forms such as "$@" preserve each original argument as its own value when forwarding to another function or command.

11
00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.420
That is different from treating the entire command line as one string or accidentally re-splitting values.

12
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:10.680
Empty arguments, values containing spaces, and values that look like options should be considered normal possibilities, not rare edge cases.

13
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.640
Argument boundaries are part of correctness and security.

14
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:18.340
A path with a space should remain one path.

15
00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.120
An empty value should be detected if it is not allowed.

16
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.900
A literal value that begins with a dash should not accidentally become an option to a downstream command.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.120
Understanding how Bash represents arguments gives reviewers the vocabulary to ask whether the script preserves what the caller actually supplied.
